== 3. The Difficulty of Duty

#strong[The third #emph[device] that Satan has to hinder souls from holy and heavenly services, from gracious and religious performances, is,]

By presenting to the soul the difficulty of performing them. Satan says, “It is so hard and difficult a thing to pray as you should, and to wait on God as you should, and to walk with God as you should, and to be lively, warm and active in the communion of saints as you should, that you would be ten thousand times better off neglecting them than meddling with them.” No doubt by this device Satan has kept, and still keeps, thousands from waiting on God, and from giving him the service that is due to his name, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell more on the necessity of the service and duty than on the difficulty that attends the duty. You#super[+] should reason with your#super[+] souls like this: “Oh, our souls! Though such and such duties and services are hard and difficult, are they not exceedingly necessary for the honor of God and the keeping up of his name in#footnote[The necessity of doing your#super[+] duty appears from this: you#super[+] are his servants by a threefold right. You#super[+] are his servants (#emph[jure creationis, jure sustentationis, jure redemptionis]) by right of creation, by right of sustaining, and by right of redemption.] the world? Are they not necessary for keeping sin under, for strengthening weak graces, for reviving languishing comforts, for keeping your#super[+] blessed evidences clear and bright, for scattering your#super[+] fears, for raising your#super[+] hopes, for gladdening the hearts of the righteous, and for stopping the mouths of unrighteous souls, who are ready to take every advantage to blaspheme the name of God and to throw dirt and contempt on his people and ways?” Oh, never stop thinking on the necessity of this and that duty, till your#super[+] souls are lifted up far above all the difficulties that attend religious duties.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that the Lord Jesus will make his services easy to you#super[+] by the sweet revealing of himself to your#super[+] souls while you#super[+] are in his service. “You meet those who gladly do right, who remember You in Your ways,” as the prophet Isaiah says. If meeting with#footnote[Isa 64:5. פגעת #emph[Paganta] is taken in various ways, but most take the word here to mean meeting a soul with that deep compassion and tenderness with which the father of the prodigal met the prodigal. God is #emph[pater miserationum] \[the Father of mercies\]; he is all compassion; he is as swift to show mercy as he is slow to anger.] God, who is goodness itself, sweetness itself, beauty itself, strength itself, glory itself, will not sweeten his service to your soul, nothing in heaven or earth will. Jacob’s meeting with Rachel, and enjoying Rachel, made his hard service easy and delightful to him. And will not the soul’s enjoying God and meeting with God make his service much more easy and delightful? No doubt it will. The Lord will give such sweet help by his Spirit and grace as will make his service joyous and not burdensome, a delight and not a burden, a heaven and not a hell, to believing souls. The confidence of this divine help raised Nehemiah’s spirit far above all the difficulties and discouragements that attended him in the work and service of the Lord, as you#super[+] may see in Neh 2:19, 20: “But when Sanballat#footnote[Luther spoke excellently to Melanchthon, who was apt to be discouraged by doubts and difficulties and fears of enemies, and so to give up the work they had undertaken: “If the work is not good, why did we ever take it up? If it is good, why should we ever turn from it? Why,” he says, “should we fear the conquered world, when we have Christ the Conqueror on our side?” Isa 40:31. “Since the head was crowned with thorns, it is not fitting that the members should be crowned with rosebuds,” says Zanchius.] the Horonite, Tobiah the Ammonite official, and Geshem the Arab heard about this, they mocked us and ridiculed us, saying, ‘What is this you#super[+] are doing? Are you#super[+] rebelling against the king?’ So I answered them and said, ‘The God of heaven will prosper us; therefore we, His servants, will arise and build. But you#super[+] have no portion, right, or claim in Jerusalem.’ ” Ah souls! While you#super[+] are in the very service of the Lord, you#super[+] will find by experience that the God of heaven will prosper you#super[+], support you#super[+], encourage and strengthen you#super[+], and carry you#super[+] through the hardest service with the greatest sweetness and cheerfulness possible. Remember this: God will suit your#super[+] strength to your#super[+] work, and in the hardest service you#super[+] will have the choicest help.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell on the hard and difficult things that the Lord Jesus Christ has gone through for your#super[+] temporal, spiritual and eternal good. Ah, what a sea of blood, a sea of wrath, of sin, of sorrow and misery the Lord Jesus waded through for your#super[+] internal and eternal good! Christ did not plead, “This cross is too heavy for me to bear, this wrath is too great for me to lie under, this cup (which has in it all the ingredients of divine displeasure) is too bitter for me to sip, let alone to drink its very dregs.” No, Christ does not insist on this. He does not plead the difficulty of the service, but resolutely and bravely wades through all, as the prophet Isaiah shows: “The Lord #smallcaps[God] has opened My ears, and I have not been rebellious, nor have I turned back. I offered My back to those who struck Me, and My cheeks to those who tore out My beard. I did not hide My face from scorn and spittle.” Christ makes nothing of his Father’s wrath, the burden of your#super[+] sins, the malice#footnote[Godfrey of Bouillon, first king of Jerusalem, refused to be crowned with a crown of gold, saying that it did not become a Christian to wear a crown of gold there, where Christ had once worn a crown of thorns for our salvation.] of Satan and the rage of the world, but sweetly and triumphantly passes through all. Ah souls! If this consideration will not raise your#super[+] spirits above all the discouragements you#super[+] meet with, to acknowledge Christ and his service, and to stick and cleave to Christ and his service, I am afraid nothing will. A soul not stirred by this, not raised and lifted up by this to be resolute and brave in the service of God in spite of all dangers and difficulties, is a soul left by God to much blindness and hardness.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that religious duties, holy and heavenly exercises, are difficult only to the worse, the ignoble part of a saint. They are not difficult to the noble and better part of a saint. To the noble part, the soul and the renewed affections of a saint, holy exercises are a heavenly pleasure and recreation, as the apostle says: “In my inner#footnote[As every flower has its sweet scent, so every good duty carries food in its mouth, that is, comfort in the doing of it. χρηστός, that is, my yoke is a kindly, a gracious, a pleasant, a good and a profitable yoke, the opposite of πονηρός, painful, tedious.] being I delight in God’s law. With my mind I serve the law of God, but with my flesh I serve the law of sin.” To the noble part of a saint, Christ’s “yoke is easy” and his “burden is light.” All the commands and ways of Christ (even those that call for plucking out right eyes and cutting off right hands) are joyous and not burdensome to the noble part of a saint. All the ways and services of Christ are pleasantness itself (in the abstract) to the better part of a saint. A saint, so far as he is renewed, is always at his best when he sees most of God, when he tastes most of God, when he is highest in his enjoyment of God, and most warm and lively in the service of God. “Oh,” says the noble part of a saint, “that it might always be like this! Oh, that my strength were the strength of stones, and my flesh bronze, so that my worse part might serve my noble part better, and that I might act with an untiring power in that service which is a pleasure, a paradise to me!”

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly the great reward and glorious recompense that attend those who cleave to the service of the Lord in the#footnote[Basil speaks of some martyrs who were thrown out naked all night in a cold, frosty season, and were to be burned the next day, and of how they comforted themselves like this: “The winter is sharp, but paradise is sweet; here we shiver with cold, but the bosom of Abraham will make amends for all.”] face of all difficulties and discouragements. Though the work is hard, yet the wages are great. Heaven will make amends for all; indeed, one hour in heaven will abundantly repay you for cleaving to the Lord and his ways in the face of all difficulties. This carried the apostle through the greatest difficulties: he had an eye to the recompense of the reward; he looked for a house that had foundations, whose builder and maker was God, and for a heavenly country. Indeed, this bore up the spirit of Christ in the face of all difficulties and discouragements: “Looking to Jesus, the author and perfecter of our faith, who for the joy set before Him endured the cross, scorning its shame, and sat down at the right hand of the throne of God.”

Christians who would hold on in the service of the Lord must look more on the crown than on the cross, more on their future glory than on their present misery, more on their encouragements than on their discouragements. God’s very service is wages. His ways are strewn with roses, and paved with joy that is unspeakable#footnote[This is #emph[praemium ante praemium] \[a reward before the reward\], a sure reward of well-doing: in doing it (not only for doing it) there is great reward, Ps 19:11.] and full of glory, and with peace that passes understanding. Some degree of comfort follows every good action, as heat accompanies fire, as beams and influences issue from the sun. “By them indeed Your servant is warned; in keeping them is great reward.” Not only for keeping them, but in keeping them, there is great reward. The joy, the rest, the refreshing, the comforts, the contentments, the smiles, the inflowings that saints now enjoy in the ways of God are so precious and glorious in their eyes that they would not exchange them for ten thousand worlds. Ah, if the tips are this sweet and glorious before payday comes, what will that glory be with which Christ will crown his saints for cleaving to his service in the face of all difficulties, when he says to his Father, “Here am I, and the children You have given Me”? If there is so much to be had in a wilderness, what then will be had in paradise? etc.
